l "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070963/state.json" "file_sha256" "73c9035a0c8c2301d3f67ebb1272dc4291a7e5e555a11b5be4bc720a9be3256e"} {"question" "q-332716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{2}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =9\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{2}^{-t}+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{2}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070963/state.json" "file_sha256" "73c9035a0c8c2301d3f67ebb1272dc4291a7e5e555a11b5be4bc720a9be3256e"} {"question"